    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate looking to leverage elite speed and favorable matchups against sub-5.00 xERA arms. The core of our offensive output today is anchored by **Corbin Carroll** (79.46) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.91). **Corbin Carroll** remains a must-start given his proximity to the top of the order and the favorable matchup against Abbott, while **Tyler Soderstrom** benefits significantly from the platoon advantage against Sugano, yielding one of our highest daily efficiency scores. **Jose Caballero** also presents an elite profile (75.83) as his dynamic platoon edge against Corbin allows him to capitalize on a soft matchup, serving as a pillar for our stolen base accumulation strategy.\n\nWe are navigating a few high-risk profiles in the interest of balancing game caps. **Seiya Suzuki** (44.77) and **Dalton Rushing** (47.18) are our most marginal starts; **Seiya Suzuki** is neutralized by a difficult matchup against Logan Webb, though he remains active to protect our counting stats. **Austin Riley** (53.34) landed as our final utility start, narrowly edging out depth options. While his projection is dampened by Peralta's presence on the mound, his status as a lineup mainstay ensures we aren't burning roster spots on volatile reserves. We are keeping a close eye on the bench, where **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Mickey Moniak**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, and **Trevor Story** remain sidelined on the IL, leaving us thin on bench depth should we need to pinch-hit for lower-scoring starters like **Max Muncy** (57.67) or **Kyle Karros** (54.63) later in the day.\n\nThe most intriguing tactical decision today involved the bottom of our infield. **Cole Young** (63.25) secured his spot as our leadoff hitter over other marginal options due to his high-leverage platoon edge against Poulin, which adds significant value to his run-scoring potential. Conversely, **Alex Freeland** (51.01) sits on the lower end of our active requirements; despite the favorable wind conditions, the combination of a weaker batting order spot and the matchup against Hudson limits his ceiling. Managers should note that while **James Wood** (65.20) draws a tough arm in Hancock, his elite xwOBA (0.403) and primary position in the order keep him firmly in the \"Strong\" start category. We remain focused on these high-upside plays to maintain our edge in the steals and runs categories.",
